---
export const prerender = true;
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import FounderPhoto from '../components/FounderPhoto.astro';
import Certifications from '../components/Certifications.astro';
import NewsletterSignup from '../components/NewsletterSignup.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — TomProtects"
  description="Tom Smith, security advisor with 20+ years of experience (CISSP, CISM, CDPSE, PMP). I help online businesses protect what they've built."
  footerNewsletter={false}
>
  <Section width="text">
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-6 sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:gap-8">
      <FounderPhoto class="h-40 w-40 shrink-0" loading="eager" />

      <div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">About</h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink/70">
          I'm Tom. I've spent more than twenty years keeping organizations secure, and I help people
          who built a business on their expertise protect what they've built.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        I work with coaches, consultants, creators, and course sellers who run a real business
        online. Your income depends on a handful of accounts: Stripe, YouTube, your email platform,
        your domain. Some of them are shared with a VA, an editor, or a contractor. They were
        probably set up one at a time, in a hurry, and nobody has looked at them together.
      </p>

      <p>
        I do this because people like you did the hard part. You built something real on your
        expertise, and the tools it runs on rarely explain themselves. Most security advice makes
        that worse. It's written for companies with an IT team, or it's written to scare you. I
        don't sell with fear, and I don't think you should have to become a security expert to
        protect your business.
      </p>

      <p>
        So I work alongside you. We go through your setup together, in plain language, and make
        the changes together, so you understand what we did and why. You stay logged in, and you
        keep control of your accounts. I work with you, not instead of you.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some people call this kind of work a fractional CISO: a part-time security leader you call
        on when you need one, rather than hire full time. The title matters less than the job.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section background="white" width="text">
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Background</h2>

    <div class="mt-6 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        Much of my career has been spent as the sole security and systems lead for a group of
        affiliated operating companies, responsible for everything from the systems people used
        every day to the security decisions nobody else was going to make. I have handled real
        incidents, not only planned for them.
      </p>

      <p>
        I also see this up close at home. My wife is an independent coach and facilitator, so I
        know what it looks like when a business runs on one person's expertise and a handful of
        accounts.
      </p>

      <p>
        That shapes how I advise. It is straightforward to recommend good security when someone
        else has to find the time and money for it. Having made those calls myself, with a limited
        budget, a small team, and a business that had to keep running while security improved,
        keeps my advice grounded in what can actually be done.
      </p>

      <h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Certifications</h3>

      <p>
        CISSP, CISM, CDPSE, and PMP. They are worth stating plainly because they show the work has
        been measured against a standard. Judgment matters more than any of them, and no
        certification demonstrates that on its own.
      </p>

      <Certifications size={52} label="Certification badges, verified on Credly" class="pt-1" />
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section width="text">
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">What makes this different</h2>

    <div class="mt-6 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        Most security advice fails for one of two reasons. It's either too generic to act on, or
        it's written for a company ten times your size, with a team to carry it out. Useful advice
        depends on knowing which parts apply to a business like yours and which can wait.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the work is practical. Recommendations come with an order, a reason, and a realistic
        sense of effort. I explain them in plain language, so you can answer a client who asks
        about your security practices, or fill out a cyber insurance form, without a translator.
        And because I've worked on both the operational side and the technical side, I can talk to
        you and to the VA or contractor making the changes without either conversation getting
        lost.
      </p>

      <p>
        I'm not here to sell you software, and I'm not going to scare you into buying anything.
        The goal is a business that knows where it stands, has a plan you believe in, and can
        explain both to anyone who asks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section background="ink" width="text">
    <NewsletterSignup id="about" variant="panel" onDark buttonLabel="Send me the checklist" />
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
